│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ seeded_hillclimb_mintriangle                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The original program returned an empty configuration. Instead of copying lattice-based    │
│                          │ constructions (which create collinear triples with zero area) or pure random search, I    │
│                          │ use a hybrid: a deterministic, seeded stochastic hill-climb over the 11 point coordinates │
│                          │ that directly maximizes the minimum triangle area (computed vectorized over all 165       │
│                          │ triplets). Key insights: (1) soft-start from a spread-out interior configuration rather   │
│                          │ than a lattice to avoid degenerate collinear triples; (2) fold points back into the       │
│                          │ triangle via barycentric clamping so every perturbation stays feasible; (3) geometric     │
│                          │ step-size annealing to refine from coarse exploration to fine-tuning. The method is fully │
│                          │ deterministic via a fixed seed and runs fast since each evaluation is a vectorized        │
│                          │ 165-triplet area computation.                                                             │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0073                                                                                   │
│ error_attempt            │ None                                                                                      │
