│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ targeted_greedy_min_triangle_search                                                       │
│ patch_description        │ The current simulated annealing moves a random point with a random perturbation —         │
│                          │ extremely inefficient since most moves don't affect the minimal triangle. The key insight │
│                          │ from Heilbronn optimization: the objective is a max-min problem, and the most effective   │
│                          │ local search directly targets the vertices of the currently smallest triangle, pushing    │
│                          │ them away from its centroid (which enlarges that specific triangle) and also testing      │
│                          │ small boundary/lateral moves. Combined with multiple deterministic restarts from          │
│                          │ lattice-like seeds and geometric step decay, this converges much closer to the known      │
│                          │ optimum (~0.0365 normalized). I also vectorize the min-area computation fully and add a   │
│                          │ final "polish" phase with tiny steps.                                                     │
