╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ softmin_then_hard_polish                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ Two improvements inspired by the Heilbronn optimization landscape:                        │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 1. **Precompute triplet indices at module level.** The current `_min_area` rebuilds the   │
│                          │ 165-combination index array on *every* call (40k+ times), which dominates the 3.87s       │
│                          │ runtime. Hoisting it to module level (as Gen 3/6 did) cuts per-iteration cost             │
│                          │ substantially, letting us afford more optimization within the same time.                  │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ 2. **Soft-min coarse phase, then hard-min polish.** The hard `min` objective is extremely │
│                          │ rugged — a single-point perturbation often leaves the argmin triangle unchanged (flat     │
│                          │ plateau) or jumps discontinuously, so greedy descent stalls (Gen 2, current program       │
│                          │ plateau at ~0.66). Gen 3 showed a log-sum-exp soft-min over the smallest ~15% of          │
│                          │ triangles smooths the landscape and guides points toward balanced configurations. We add  │
│                          │ a coarse phase minimizing `logsumexp(-k*a)/k` over the smallest triangles with            │
│                          │ simulated-annealing acceptance, then keep the existing (cheapened) hard-min annealing +   │
│                          │ fine polish to sharpen the actual minimum.                                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This combines Gen 3's smoothing with the current program's better schedule and clipping.  │
│ num_applied              │ 3                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.0086                                                                                   │
