│ patch_name               │ coordinated_triple_moves                                                                  │
│ patch_description        │ The recommendation section suggests the top programs saturate because single-point moves  │
│                          │ cannot escape configurations where the smallest triangle is "trapped". I add coordinated  │
│                          │ group moves to the polish phase: with ~20% probability, simultaneously perturb all 3      │
│                          │ vertices of the current smallest-area triangle with correlated Gaussian deltas scaled to  │
│                          │ the current step. Moving all three vertices together lets the triangle "translate/expand" │
│                          │ as a unit — something impossible when moving points one at a time against a hard-min      │
│                          │ objective, since any single-vertex move typically increases some other triangle's         │
│                          │ deficiency first. Additionally, I bias the correlated component along the outward         │
│                          │ direction from the triangle's centroid (its natural expansion mode), which directly       │
│                          │ increases its area while the shared translation component keeps other areas roughly       │
│                          │ invariant. I also track the best-so-far configuration independently from the annealing    │
│                          │ current state, so worsening (escape) moves never destroy the incumbent. This targets      │
│                          │ pushing min_area from the ~0.01–0.03 plateau toward the ~0.037 benchmark.                 │
