│ patch_name               │ pair_exchange_escape                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Add a final coordinate-descent "pair-exchange" perturbation phase after the fine polish,  │
│                          │ following the recommendation. All top programs plateau at ~0.03 (vs. the ~0.037-0.040     │
│                          │ optimum), suggesting trapped local minima where the binding vertex of the smallest        │
│                          │ triangle cannot improve by continuous moves. The structured move: identify the vertices   │
│                          │ of the smallest triangles ("binding" points) and swap one with a "free" point whose       │
│                          │ incident triangle areas are all comfortably large, then re-run a short hard-min polish    │
│                          │ (decaying steps 0.01→1e-4, ~3k iters) from the swapped configuration. Keep the result     │
│                          │ only if the true best improves. Per-vertex binding scores are computed cheaply with the   │
│                          │ existing vectorized `_all_areas` machinery via `np.minimum.reduceat`-style accumulation   │
│                          │ over the (165,3) triplet index array. This restarts the local search in a new basin       │
│                          │ without sacrificing the incumbent. Runtime increases modestly (~2000 swap attempts ×      │
│                          │ short polish would be too slow, so we limit to ~12 accepted swap trials with              │
│                          │ 2500-iteration polishes, keeping eval_time acceptable while giving a real chance of       │
│                          │ escaping the plateau).                                                                    │
