│ patch_name               │ directed_normal_sweep_polish                                                              │
│ patch_description        │ The best prior variants score ~0.80 using random + coordinate polish. The key weakness:   │
│                          │ the greedy coordinate polish moves points along axes only and the random walk spends most │
│                          │ moves on points not in any binding triangle. I add a deterministic directed polish that   │
│                          │ (a) identifies the ~10 smallest triangles each pass, (b) for each binding vertex probes   │
│                          │ moves along the outward normal directions of the two edges adjacent to that vertex inside │
│                          │ the small triangle (moving a vertex away from the opposite edge increases that triangle's │
│                          │ area fastest), and (c) uses step-halving on failure. Additionally, keep 20% random kicks  │
│                          │ (via a fixed RNG) so the sweep retains exploration. This directly targets the binding     │
│                          │ constraints that define the objective, which coordinate-axis moves miss.                  │
│ num_applied              │ 2                                                                                         │
