│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ subgradient_ascent_heilbronn11                                                            │
│ patch_description        │ Fundamentally different approach: instead of blind stochastic hill-climbing on a soft     │
│                          │ surrogate, use directed subgradient ascent on the hard objective. At each iteration we    │
│                          │ identify all near-minimal triangles, compute the exact analytic gradient of their         │
│                          │ (signed) area with respect to each vertex, and move only the vertices involved in the     │
│                          │ bottleneck triangles in the direction that increases the minimum. This is combined with   │
│                          │ (1) a short simulated-annealing exploration phase using coordinated group moves, (2) many │
│                          │ structured boundary-heavy seeds (known to be good for Heilbronn n=11), and (3) a          │
│                          │ barycentric parameterization with a safety validation harness that falls back to a        │
│                          │ lattice seed on any failure. Directed gradient moves converge much faster than random     │
│                          │ perturbation, so we can afford many restarts within the time budget.                      │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
